## Summary

A config update returns before the nested entries it created have been reconciled, so `await loader.update(...)` resolves while the new child is still being imported.

Upstream cordis fixed exactly this in [`1b7d0f2`](https://github.com/cordiverse/cordis/commit/1b7d0f263b5345d6c36aa9ff48cd6fe525147726) — *fix(loader): await nested reconciliation during config update* (2026-09-03), which also added a test for it in `packages/loader/tests/group.spec.ts`. The vendored line in `@deepseek-ai/cordis-plugin-loader` does not have that change.

## Where

`cordis-plugin-loader/src/config/entry.ts`, `Entry._patchContext`:

```ts
private _patchContext(diff: string[]) {
  this.context.waterfall('loader/patch-context', this, () => {
    Object.setPrototypeOf(this.ctx, this.parent.ctx)

    if (this.fiber?.uid && (diff.includes('config') || this.options.group)) {
      this.fiber.update(this.options.config, true)
    }
  })
}
```

The method is not `async` and neither the waterfall nor `fiber.update` is awaited, so all three call sites proceed immediately. Upstream made the method async and awaits both:

```ts
private async _patchContext(diff: string[]) {
  await this.context.waterfall('loader/patch-context', this, async () => {
    ...
      await this.fiber.update(this._resolveConfig(this.fiber.runtime!.callback), true)
  })
}
```

## Reproduction

```js
import { Context } from '@deepseek-ai/cordis'
import { Loader, Group } from '@deepseek-ai/cordis-plugin-loader'

const sleep = (ms = 0) => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms))
const root = new Context()
await root.plugin(Loader)
const loader = root.loader

const foo = () => {}
const bar = () => {}
loader.modules = { foo, bar }                       // stand in for real plugins
loader.import = async (name) => { await sleep(20); return loader.modules[name] }

const group = await loader.create({
  name: '@cordisjs/plugin-group',
  group: true,
  config: [{ id: '1', name: 'foo' }],
})
await sleep(0)

await loader.update(group, {
  config: [{ id: '1', name: 'foo' }, { id: '2', name: 'bar' }],
})

// upstream: both already true / present when update() resolves
console.log('bar enabled:', !!root.registry.get(bar))
console.log('entry "2" fiber:', !!loader.store['2']?.fiber)
```

The 20 ms import is only there to make the gap observable; the ordering is wrong regardless of how slow the import is.

| | `bar` enabled | entry `"2"` fiber |
|---|---|---|
| `@deepseek-ai/cordis-plugin-loader` (as shipped) | `false` | absent |
| upstream `cordis@4.0.0-rc.10` | `true` | present |

Same probe against both, unchanged.

## Why it matters here

The upstream test spells out the consequence: *"without the awaited chain the update resolves while the new child is still being imported."* Anything that awaits a config change and then uses the entries it just declared is racing — enabling or disabling plugins, and reloading a config file, both go through this path.

## Two more upstream changes the vendored line does not have

Not part of the same defect, but they come from the same window and touch the same files, so I checked them while I was in there:

- **`#121`** — [`c594d1a`](https://github.com/cordiverse/cordis/commit/c594d1a) *fix(include): reconcile file and runtime edits through a journal*. `cordis-plugin-include` here has no journal at all, and the same commit also reordered `EntryGroup.remove` so the entry is unregistered **before** the fiber is disposed — with a comment explaining that the `internal/plugin` handler tells "removed by the loader" from "disposed itself" by checking whether the entry is still in the store. The vendored `remove` still disposes first and omits the `entry.parent !== this` guard the same commit added.
- **`#123`** — [`c2835d8`](https://github.com/cordiverse/cordis/commit/c2835d8) *fix(loader): resolve bare specifiers from the project*. There is no `resolve.ts` in the vendored loader, so bare specifiers in a config file resolve against the loader rather than the project.

I have not reproduced the second one, so I am reporting it as a source-level difference rather than a confirmed defect.

## Related

I have been sending fixes for some of the same files upstream — [#164–#172](https://github.com/cordiverse/cordis/pulls?q=is%3Apr+author%3A173787247) and [#173](https://github.com/cordiverse/cordis/pull/173), [#174](https://github.com/cordiverse/cordis/pull/174) — several with regression tests, if any of this is easier to act on with a patch attached.

**Summary**

In the `tui` profile, a freshly created session exposes 27 tools, including `ask_user_question` and `present`. Resume that same session (`dsh --profile tui --resume <id>`, or `/resume <id>` inside the TUI) and it comes back with 25 — exactly those two gone, for the rest of the session's life. Only a *new* session gets them again.

**Repro**

1. `dsh --profile tui`, start a session — tool list has 27 entries, `ask_user_question` and `present` included.
2. Quit, then `dsh --profile tui --resume <id>` (or `/resume <id>` from inside).
3. The resumed session's tool list has 25; both tools are missing and never come back.

Reproducible on every resume, not intermittently: 20 sessions / 25 resume events on this machine, no exceptions. The session log records it — `request/header` events carry the model-facing tool list, and a header is only appended when it differs from the previously logged one, so the 25s are the real composed set, not a logging artefact.

**Root cause**

`@tomowang/dsh-tui`'s `attachSession()` (`lib/index.js`, ~lines 803-830) resolves the preset only on the fresh-session branch:

```js
let resolvedPreset;
let presetNotice;
if (resumeId === undefined) {                    // fresh sessions only
    if (presets !== undefined) {
        try { resolvedPreset = await presets.resolve(config.agentPreset); }
        catch (error) { presetNotice = … }
    }
    …
}
const setup = async (agentCtx) => {
    const selected = { current: selection, assembled: undefined };
    installModelSelection(agentCtx, selected);
    if (presets !== undefined && resolvedPreset !== undefined)
        await presets.mount(agentCtx, resolvedPreset.id);   // unreachable on resume
};
const { agent, dispose } = resumeId !== undefined
    ? await agents.resume({ resumeSessionId: …, agentOptions, setup })
    : await agents.create({ …, setup });
```

So a resumed agent is booted with a `setup` that installs the model selection and never joins a preset. `AgentPresets.mount()` is what binds the agent's scope to the standing composition (`bindScopeParent(agentKey, standing.key)`), and the only other join path is `composeFrom()`, which exists for child agents joining a parent. `@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-presets` detects and warns about exactly this state from its own `agent/created` listener:

> agent "…" was published without joining an agent preset; its tools, prompt sections, and skill catalog resolve against the empty global layer (join through AgentPresets.mount() or composeFrom() in the agent factory setup)

The comment directly above the guard says the resumed session's preset is "whatever its persisted header (or a later `agent-preset/selected` event) already recorded" — but nothing on this path reads that projection and mounts it. The data is available and built for reconstruction: the `agentPreset` projection is `init: (header) => header.agentPreset ?? null`, documented as "Reconstruction reads the `agentPreset` Session projection, never the header".

**Why only those two tools look broken**

`dsh-base` host-mounts its own copies of nearly every model-facing row, so the global layer still yields 25 tools and the resumed session looks almost normal. Comparing row sets, only three rows exist in `presets/standard/agent.cordis.yml` and nowhere in `dsh-base`:

- `@deepseek-ai/dsh-persona`
- `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-ask-user` → `ask_user_question`
- `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-present` → `present`

The persona row has a near-identical host-side counterpart in the TUI bundle, which masks it. A user-authored preset would presumably lose **every** preset-only row on resume — tools, prompt sections and skill catalog — with no error and no notice.

**Compare**

`@deepseek-ai/dsh-api-session-controller`'s `composeAgent()`, documented as "Resolve the preset id and pre-publication Agent setup for a **create or resume**", always resolves and always mounts:

```js
const resolvedId = (await presets.resolve(presetId)).id;
return { agentPreset: resolvedId, setup: async (agentCtx, agent) => {
    this.installSelection(agent);
    await presets.mount(agentCtx, resolvedId);
} };
```

`@deepseek-ai/dsh-webhook` likewise. `dsh-tui` is the only `mount()` caller that branches on resume — and the only one that loses the rows.

**Suggested fix**

Join the persisted preset on resume. Ideally in the agent resume path (read the `agentPreset` projection), so no caller can forget it. Minimal TUI-side alternative: resolve the persisted preset for `resumeId` before `agents.resume` and set `resolvedPreset` from it so the existing `setup` mounts it.

**Environment**

- dsh `0.1.5-rc.3`
- `@tomowang/dsh-tui` `0.8.1`
- `@earendil-works/pi-tui` `0.84.4`
- `tui` profile (bundles: `@deepseek-ai/dsh-base`, `@tomowang/dsh-tui`, plus local plugins)
- Linux (EndeavourOS), agent preset `standard`

# Summary

`LocalFileSystem.watch()` creates a separate Chokidar watcher on the **parent directory** for each file target. The `ignored` predicate excludes unrelated entries only after the parent has been enumerated. Concurrent watches on sibling files therefore allocate multiple complete listings of the same directory. With a large temporary directory this can exhaust the host V8 heap.

The allocation amplification is reproduced independently of DSH startup, session history, or third-party plugins. It strongly matches two observed `dsh web --no-open` crashes, although the precise subscriptions that triggered those crashes could not be recovered.

## Environment

- DSH **0.1.7-rc.2** (`dsh-v0.1.7-rc.2`)
- Linux, Nix-packaged installation
- Node.js **24.19.0**
- Installed Chokidar used in reproduction: **4.0.3**
- A real `/tmp` directory containing approximately **343,500 entries**

## Implementation

[packages/fs/fs-local/src/index.ts, lines 69–78](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/blob/dsh-v0.1.7-rc.2/packages/fs/fs-local/src/index.ts#L69-L78):

```ts
const directory = (await this.stat(target, signal))?.type === 'directory'
const root = directory ? path : dirname(path)
const watcher = watch(root, {
  ignoreInitial: true,
  depth: 0,
  ignored: entry => !directory && resolve(entry) !== root && resolve(entry) !== path,
})
```

Neither `ignoreInitial: true` nor `depth: 0` avoids enumerating the root directory. Chokidar/readdirp reads the directory before applying the entry filters. Each independent watcher repeats that work.

[WorkspaceChangeFeed.follow](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/blob/dsh-v0.1.7-rc.2/packages/api/workspace-files/src/changes.ts#L79-L82) opens a filesystem watch for each follower. The [client change feed](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/blob/dsh-v0.1.7-rc.2/packages/api/workspace-files/src/client/change-feed.ts#L278-L290) shares by session and requested path, not by parent directory. Thus different file targets in one directory are not coalesced.

## Isolated reproduction and measurements

Using existing regular files in the large directory, start multiple watchers concurrently with the exact parent-directory pattern above. No DSH instance or custom plugins are needed. Run each case in a separate Node process.

Measured with `node --max-old-space-size=256 --expose-gc`, core dumps disabled, a 40-second deadline, and an external 650 MiB RSS safety cutoff:

| Setup | Full directory reads | Peak sampled JS heap | Peak sampled RSS | Result |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
| Parent-directory pattern, 1 file | 1 | ~35 MiB | ~135 MiB | Ready in ~4.2 s |
| Parent-directory pattern, 4 files | 4 | ~127 MiB | ~333 MiB | Ready in ~17.5 s |
| Parent-directory pattern, 16 files | Not completely measured before abort | Exhausted 256 MiB limit | ~580 MiB | V8 heap OOM |
| Direct-file watching, 16 files | **0** | ~5 MiB | ~55 MiB | Ready in ~2 ms |

Four watchers enumerated approximately **1.37 million entries**, despite watching only four files. After successful initialization and explicit GC, the 1- and 4-watcher cases returned to approximately 5 MiB heap. This is **transient concurrent-listing amplification**, not evidence that completed watchers permanently retain all entries.

The A/B comparison changed only watcher construction to:

```js
watch(file, { ignoreInitial: true, depth: 0 })
```

### Minimal reproducer outline

Run where `require("chokidar")` resolves to Chokidar 4.0.3 (for example, from the installed `fs-local` package). Save the following as `watch-repro.cjs` and pass distinct existing sibling file paths in a directory with hundreds of thousands of entries:

```js
const { watch } = require("chokidar");
const { dirname, resolve } = require("node:path");
const { once } = require("node:events");

const files = process.argv.slice(2).map(p => resolve(p));
if (!files.length) throw new Error("Pass existing sibling file paths");
const direct = process.env.MODE === "direct";
const started = Date.now();
const sample = () => console.log(Date.now() - started, process.memoryUsage());
const timer = setInterval(sample, 100);

(async () => {
  const watchers = files.map(file => {
    const root = dirname(file);
    return direct
      ? watch(file, { ignoreInitial: true, depth: 0 })
      : watch(root, {
          ignoreInitial: true,
          depth: 0,
          ignored: entry => resolve(entry) !== root && resolve(entry) !== file,
        });
  });
  try {
    await Promise.all(watchers.map(w => once(w, "ready")));
    console.log("ready");
    sample();
    global.gc?.();
    sample();
  } finally {
    clearInterval(timer);
    await Promise.all(watchers.map(w => w.close()));
  }
})().catch(error => { console.error(error); process.exitCode = 1; });
```

```sh
# Use a disposable/isolated process; the parent-mode case intentionally stresses memory.
# Supply the same 16 existing sibling paths in each command. No files are written.
ulimit -c 0
timeout 40s node --max-old-space-size=256 --expose-gc watch-repro.cjs <paths...>
MODE=direct timeout 40s node --max-old-space-size=256 --expose-gc watch-repro.cjs <paths...>
```

The snippet illustrates the reproduced pattern; the measurements above came from an instrumented driver that additionally counted `readdir` calls and sampled RSS externally. Timing and thresholds depend on directory size and the environment.

## Original crash evidence and limits

- Two host crashes with `FATAL ERROR: Reached heap limit Allocation failed - JavaScript heap out of memory`, near the normal ~4 GiB heap limit. One process lasted only about 203 seconds.
- The observed native crash stack included `node::fs::AfterScanDir`, `v8::String::NewFromOneByte`, and `v8::internal::Factory::NewStringFromOneByte`.
- A truncated 2 GiB core contained **9,220,574 matches for `nix-develop-*` basenames**, representing **80,499 distinct names**. Typical multiplicities were roughly 108–121, with a maximum of 131. Sampled high-frequency names exist in `/tmp`. This is repeated directory-name data, not millions of distinct files.
- The core was truncated, so the originating native filesystem request and exact active GUI subscriptions were not recovered. The isolated reproduction established OOM, but its full native stderr stack was not retained; I am not claiming its stack was independently verified to match `AfterScanDir`.
- There is an automatic subscription path through document previews and their related resources, but no evidence proves which preview/resource triggered the observed incidents. In particular, this report does **not** assume that `read_image` results or ordinary inline links automatically open live file watches, or that the user clicked anything.

## Expected behavior / possible repair

Watching a few files should not allocate one complete parent-directory listing per file. Consider direct-file watching where semantics permit, or sharing/coalescing parent-directory watches with bounded initialization work.

The direct-file comparison is a promising repair direction, **not a validated drop-in fix**. Please cover missing targets, deletion/recreation, atomic replacement, cancellation during initialization, and watcher disposal before adopting it. Raising the host heap limit only moves the failure threshold.

## Related reports checked

English and Chinese Discussions searches found other OOM and watcher reports, but no report clearly describing this per-file parent-directory enumeration amplification. Related candidates include #4232 (large-workspace startup OOM), #3560 (symlink-loop traversal), #6099 (Windows skill watcher event storm), and #6511 (session-log decoding/retention). This report isolates a separate reproducible filesystem-watching mechanism rather than assuming every OOM shares one cause.

## TL;DR (EN)

On macOS desktop, `setPermissionCheckHandler` in `apps/desktop/src/microphone-permissions.ts` returns DENIED while the OS microphone status is not yet granted. Chromium's getUserMedia pre-check short-circuits on DENIED and never reaches `setPermissionRequestHandler`, so `systemPreferences.askForMediaAccess('microphone')` (the only call site in the app) never runs → the macOS permission prompt can never appear → voice input is permanently unusable for first-time users.

## 环境

- DeepSeek Harness 桌面版 `0.1.7-rc.2`(nightly 渠道),bundle id `com.deepseek.dsh`
- macOS 26.6.2 (Build 25G83),Apple Silicon (arm64)
- 语音输入插件(speech-to-text / SenseVoiceSmall INT8):模型下载与 sha256 校验均成功
- 应用的 Info.plist 已声明 `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription`

## 复现步骤(全新环境 100% 复现)

1. 全新安装桌面版,启用语音输入,按引导完成 SenseVoiceSmall(INT8)模型下载(此步成功);
2. 点击聊天输入框旁的麦克风按钮;
3. 显示「麦克风权限未开启,请在浏览器和系统设置中允许访问」;
4. 检查「系统设置 → 隐私与安全性 → 麦克风」:列表中**不存在** DeepSeek Harness,且全程**从未弹出** macOS 授权弹窗;
5. 之后每次点击麦克风均复现第 3 步,系统授权弹窗永远不会出现。

## 实际行为 vs 期望行为

- **实际**:getUserMedia 抛 `NotAllowedError`;应用从未向 macOS 发起麦克风授权请求;TCC 数据库中无本应用条目;语音输入在 macOS 桌面端对首次使用的用户完全不可用。
- **期望**:首次点击麦克风时触发 macOS 授权弹窗,用户允许后正常进入录音。

## 根因分析

`apps/desktop/src/microphone-permissions.ts`(master 分支,L17–L29)同时设置了 check 与 request 两个处理器:

```ts
session.setPermissionCheckHandler((contents, permission, origin, details) => {
  ...
  && (process.platform !== 'darwin'
      || systemPreferences.getMediaAccessStatus('microphone') === 'granted')   // ← 问题点
});

session.setPermissionRequestHandler((contents, permission, callback, details) => {
  ...
  void systemPreferences.askForMediaAccess('microphone').then(callback, () => callback(false))  // ← 全应用唯一的弹窗入口
});
```

问题链:

1. macOS 麦克风状态为 not determined(首次使用)或 denied 时,`getMediaAccessStatus` 非 granted,check handler 对 media 返回 **false**,即权限查询得到 **DENIED**;
2. Chromium 的 MediaStreamDevicesController 发起 getUserMedia 时先查当前权限状态,拿到 DENIED 后**直接失败**,不再进入 request 流程;
3. 于是 `setPermissionRequestHandler`(内含唯一的 `askForMediaAccess` 调用)永远不会执行 → 系统授权弹窗永远无法触发 → TCC 无条目 → check 永远返回 false。

死锁:**未授权 → check DENIED → 直接拒绝 → 永不弹窗 → 永远无法授权**。

## 建议修复方向(任一即可)

1. check handler 不应把「未授权(not determined)」映射为 DENIED:对来自 `dsh-app://app` 主框架的 audio 请求,在状态非明确 denied 时放行/返回 ASK 语义,让请求进入 `setPermissionRequestHandler`,由其调用 `askForMediaAccess` 完成首次系统授权;
2. 或在语音输入插件启用/首次点击时,由主进程显式调用一次 `systemPreferences.askForMediaAccess('microphone')` 完成授权引导,之后再依赖 check handler 的 granted 状态;
3. 附带改进:当 `askForMediaAccess` 返回 false 且系统状态为 denied 时,前端给出「打开系统设置」的可点击引导(当前文案「请在浏览器和系统设置中允许访问」对桌面端用户有误导,让人误以为是浏览器问题)。

## 临时缓解(供其他用户参考)

- 若「系统设置 → 隐私与安全性 → 麦克风」列表底部有「+」,可手动添加 DeepSeek Harness 并启用;系统状态变为 granted 后 check handler 放行,语音输入立即可用;
- 若列表无「+」(如本机),目前没有用户侧解法,只能等修复。

0.1.7-rc.2。profile 里注册了 10 个 preset，会话用的是内置 standard，一个自定义模式都没选中，但每步都在为它们等时间。

实测（同一会话，取 session.v4.jsonl.zstd 里相邻 step/end → step/start 的间隔）：

注册 0 个 → 1.22s/步
注册 1 个 → 1.45s/步
注册 4 个 → 1.71s/步
注册 10 个 → 3.43s/步

每多注册一个约 +0.22s/步，闲置的照样收费。一个 10 步的任务，9 个没用上的 preset 白扔 20 秒。

packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/lib/index.js：

async register(definition) {
    ...
    record.ready = this.activate(record);   // 注册里直接激活
    await record.ready;
}

async activate(record) {
    const generation = {
        scope, key,
        mount: await mountPreset(...),       // 激活里直接挂载整棵树
        users: 0,                            // 挂载时使用者就是 0
        retired: false
    };
    ...
}

async collect(generation) {
    if (!generation.retired || generation.users !== 0) return;   // 只回收 retired 的
    ...
}

retired 全文件只在 unregister() 里赋值一次，也就是只有声明方插件主动注销才会置位。所以注册过的 preset 一旦挂上就永远留着，没有路径释放。

而要让 preset 出现在选择器里又必须 register（list() 只读 this.definitions），绕不开。

users 引用计数其实已经实现了（会话绑定/解绑时增减），只是没用来触发卸载。

建议把 mountPreset 推迟到第一个 users++，users 归零时 collect；简单点的做法是让 collect 也回收 users === 0 的非 retired 世代，下次 users++ 时重建。

复现：往 cordis.patch.yml 里插 N 条 @deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset，开一个用内置 standard 的会话做几次工具调用，解压 session.v4.jsonl.zstd 取相邻 step/end 和 step/start 的 time 差。

# `New session` is a silent no-op when the target workspace has no sessions (0.1.5-rc.3, works on 0.1.7-rc.2)

## Summary

On the **stable channel** (`latest` = `0.1.5-rc.3`), clicking **New session** (sidebar) — or a workspace-row new-session entry — does **nothing at all** when the target workspace currently has **no sessions** (e.g. a freshly added workspace). No session is created, no navigation happens, no error is shown, and **not a single RPC leaves the client**. There is not even a console warning, so the button looks alive (hover/click work) but is functionally dead.

The same reproduction on the **next channel** (`next` = `0.1.7-rc.2`) works: the click produces `POST /api/session/create` and a session is created. So this looks like a 0.1.5-rc.3-only regression that is already fixed on `next` — the ask is to **backport/confirm the fix on the stable channel**, and at minimum stop swallowing the failure.

## Environment

- DSH: `@deepseek-ai/dsh@0.1.5-rc.3` (global npm install; `latest` channel at the time of writing)
- Also reproduced on: `@deepseek-ai/dsh@0.1.7-rc.2` (fixed)
- Profile: `web` (default bundle set, browser UI at `http://127.0.0.1:3080`)
- Node: `v24.19.0` · macOS (darwin arm64) · pnpm 11.22.0
- Browser: Chromium via Playwright (headless), fresh browser profile, locale `zh-CN` — so no stale client bundle / cache involvement

## Reproduction

Two ways; the second is deterministic and version-comparable.

### A. Manual (what a user sees)

1. Boot the web profile: `dsh web`.
2. Add a **new, empty workspace** (a directory with no DSH sessions yet).
3. Make sure this workspace has zero sessions in the sidebar.
4. Click **New session** (sidebar) — or the workspace row's new-session entry.
5. Nothing happens. No new row appears, the center pane does not change, the console stays clean.

### B. Deterministic, no clicks needed

```bash
# isolated home + isolated browser profile, so the user's own state is untouched
export DSH_HOME=/tmp/dsh-repro-home
