In `ToolRuntime`, the monotonic guards (`guardReason`) run once — after the `tools/pre-execute` listeners and **before** the around-dispatch `tools/execute` wrappers. A wrapper can await (timeouts, retries, metrics); if an embedder's policy changes during that await (a permission revoked, a session's approval stance tightened), the tool body still runs on the earlier allow.

Checked on `dsh-v0.2.0-rc.1`: `packages/core/tools/src/index.ts` evaluates `guardReason` in the scheduler gate (~L1519) but not at body admission (~L1577, just before `state.bodyInvoked = true`).

The fix we run in our embedding re-evaluates the guards at body entry. Guards are deny-only, so running them twice can never turn a denial into an allow:

```ts
exec.signal = signal
try {
  // Wrappers may await after preparation. Revalidate at body admission so
  // a policy change during that wait cannot authorize a new side effect.
  const denialReason = this.guardReason(exec)
  if (denialReason !== undefined) return toolErrorResult(new Error(denialReason))
  const tool = this.resolveExecution(exec.name, exec.agent, exec.parent !== undefined)
```

…plus a JSDoc note on the guard contract that guards may run more than once per call and must read current policy.

We have tests for it — a subcall revoked during its execution wrapper (completed siblings preserved), a scoped guard rechecked after a wrapper waits (another agent unaffected), and a replacement guard applied at body admission after the preparation traversal — happy to share them if useful.

