### What happens

`dsh-acp` gives the reasoning and the reply of one assistant message the same `messageId`. An ACP client that groups chunks by `messageId` treats both as one message, and the reasoning text appears inside the reply.

Seen in Paseo (0.9.2 and 0.10.x) through the DeepSeek Harness provider plugin: the full reasoning is shown again in the reply body.

### Where

`@deepseek-ai/dsh-acp` `lib/index.js`, `assistantUpdates` (0.2.0-rc.1, also 0.1.7-rc.2):

```js
if (block.type === "reasoning") {
  updates.push({
    sessionUpdate: "agent_thought_chunk",
    messageId: event.data.message.id,   // same id
    content: { type: "text", text: block.text },
  });
  continue;
}
updates.push({
  sessionUpdate: "agent_message_chunk",
  messageId: event.data.message.id,     // same id
  content,
});
```

### Why this is a problem

ACP v1, [Prompt Turn → Message IDs](https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/prompt-turn):

> The Agent **MAY** include an opaque, unique `messageId` on message chunks. Chunks with the same `messageId` belong to the same message; a changed `messageId` indicates a new message.

A thought and a reply are different messages, but here they share one ID, so a spec-following client is told they are the same message.

### Suggested fix

Give thought chunks their own ID, for example `${message.id}:thought`, or omit `messageId` on `agent_thought_chunk`.

### Workaround on the client side

The Paseo plugin now rewrites the thought chunk ID before passing it on ([geoqiao/paseo-stuff#21](https://github.com/geoqiao/paseo-stuff/pull/21)). We will remove that once dsh-acp sends distinct IDs.

### Summary

Some MCP servers deliver their payload **only** in `structuredContent` and return an empty `content` array. The dsh MCP client does not fall back in this case, so the model receives a placeholder like `(<tool> returned no model-visible content)` even though the call succeeded server-side. From the agent's perspective this looks like a timeout / silent failure and triggers blind retries.

### Environment

- dsh CLI 0.1.7-rc.2 (0.2.0-rc.1's `dsh-mcp-client` has the same issue — verified against the npm tarball)
- Affected servers (tested): `mcp-stata` 3.x, `paper-search-mcp` (S2/OpenAlex sources)

### Reproduction

1. Connect any MCP server that responds with `structuredContent` only and an empty `content` array (e.g. `mcp-stata@3` executing a Stata regression — the job actually completes).
2. Ask the agent to run that tool.
3. Server-side logs show success; the model receives "(tool returned no model-visible content)" and assumes the call timed out, then retries.

### Expected

When `content` carries no blocks but `structuredContent` is present, the serialized structured payload should be projected as one text block so the result stays visible to the model.

### Related discussions (checked before posting)

- #7788 reports `structuredContent` being dropped *downstream* of the bridge (computer-use `element_token` never arrives) — the bridge itself preserves it there. Our case is upstream of that: the bridge receives valid `structuredContent` but the model never sees anything when `content` is empty.
- #5565 is the mirror direction (content-only results rejected when `structuredContent` is missing). Our case is a successful `structuredContent`-bearing result whose payload is invisible because no fallback projection exists.

### Suggested fix

We are running this locally as a patch (working well since 2026-09-28); it only touches results that have no content blocks:

```js
function projectStructuredFallback(content, structured) {
  if (structured === void 0) return content;
  if (Array.isArray(content) && content.length !== 0) return content;
  let text;
  try {
    text = JSON.stringify(structured, null, 2);
  } catch {
    text = "[structuredContent could not be serialized]";
  }
  if (typeof text !== "string" || text.length === 0) return content;
  return [{ type: "text", text }];
}

// call site:
content: projectStructuredFallback(content, result.structuredContent),
...result.structuredContent !== void 0 ? { structuredContent: result.structuredContent } : {},
```

Happy to send a PR if the direction is acceptable.

## TL;DR

`Entry#get disabled()` 是沿 context 链派生的：对每一层祖先的原始 `disabled`（含 `!!js` 表达式）**在当前 entry 自己的 ctx 上求值**。因此，当某个工具/面板为了"重启这一行插件"而直接调用 `fiber.dispose()` + `entry.init()`（而不是让补丁层重新应用来重建 entry）时，那条 ctx 链被重接，祖先表达式开始对该 entry 求值为 `true` —— 于是**这一行永远显示"已停用"，而它的插件其实在正常运行**。更麻烦的是这个状态**自锁**：`update()` 与 `refresh()` 都在 `if (this.disabled)` 处提前返回，所以任何 entry 自己的 API 都无法把它救回来，只能靠父级树重建（补丁层重新应用）或重启进程。

## 环境

- DeepSeek Harness `0.2.0-rc.1` / `0.2.0-rc.2`（桌面版 runtime 同为 rc.2），Windows 11
- `@deepseek-ai/cordis-plugin-loader@1.0.5`
- 触发者：一个社区插件（MCP 管理器面板）实现"重启某一行插件"时调用 `entry.fiber.dispose()` + `entry.init()`

## 实测（同一行，前后对比）

```
调用前:  entry.disabled = False   fiber.state = 2 (active)
调用后:  entry.disabled = True    fiber.state = 1 → 2 (active)
         同一时刻：该行工具全部可用、服务发布的 providerName 仍在 → 插件确实在跑
```

复现是 100% 的（多次、多个不同的行，包括服务行与其 provider 行；普通 MCP 客户端行同样中招）。

## 代码依据（1.0.5，`lib/index.js`）

```js
// L334
get disabled() {
  if (this.options.group) return false;
  let entry = this;
  do {
    if (this.disabledOf(entry.options)) return true;   // disabledOf 会用 this.ctx 求值 !!js
    entry = entry.parent.ctx.fiber.entry;
  } while (entry);
  return false;
}
// L371  update()
if (this.disabled) { this.fiber?.dispose(); return; }  // 自锁
// L361  refresh()
if (this.fiber) return;
if (this.disabled) return;                             // 自锁
```

## 与 #7922 的关系

我这边的插桩 trace 还显示：携带变更的那次 reload 会被判失败——`reconcileProfilePatches` 在 `await ctx.loader.await()` 之后立刻取 `inactiveEntries()`，把一个**瞬时** `pending (waiting for service: browserUse)` 的 provider 当作 introduced failure，抛出 `dsh: warning: 1 entry did not activate`；下一次"配置无变化"的 reload 才通过。这看起来与 #7922（config update 未等待嵌套条目 reconcile，上游 cordis `1b7d0f2` 已修）是同一根因，故此处只作佐证、不另开一帖。

## 建议

1. 让 `update()` / `refresh()` 不要因为**派生**出来的 `disabled` 而提前返回（或提供显式的"强制重建"入口），否则一行插件一旦进入该状态就再也无法自愈；
2. 在文档/API 层面明确：重启单个插件条目应当通过 patch generation 重新应用（或 `loader` 提供的等价入口）完成，而不应 `fiber.dispose()` + `entry.init()`；
3. 若可行，`disabledOf()` 是否应该对"祖先的 `!!js` 表达式"使用**祖先自己的 ctx** 求值，而不是 `this.ctx`？这可能是最根本的一处。

（本文由社区插件的作者/使用者整理，附有原始 trace 与复现步骤，需要更多细节我可以补充。）

# Upstream report — @deepseek-ai/dsh

**Channel:** `deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness` has GitHub Issues **disabled** (the API returns
`410 Issues has been disabled in this repository`). The project README routes feedback and bug
reports to GitHub Discussions, so this one was posted there, under Q&A.
**Packages:** `@deepseek-ai/dsh` 0.1.7-rc.2 observed; crash stack from `@deepseek-ai/dsh-subprocess-local` 0.1.2-rc.1
**Environment:** Windows 11, Node v22.17.0
**Severity:** Critical — one deleted temp dir kills the entire `dsh web` server and every live session

## Title

Uncaught `ENOENT` in `OutputCollector.spillAll` terminates the whole `dsh web` server when its spill dir is deleted mid-session

## Observed crash (verbatim)

```
Error: ENOENT: no such file or directory, open 'C:\Users\Prime\AppData\Local\Temp\dsh-subprocess-Kp72eM\dsh-subprocess-18596-1-8ee8c7f6420e-stdout.log'
    at openSync (node:fs:561:18)
    at OutputCollector.spillAll (file:///C:/Users/Prime/AppData/Local/npm-cache/_npx/1e7f6d9597241db0/node_modules/@deepseek-ai/dsh-subprocess-local/lib/index.js:717:19)
    at OutputCollector.push (file:///C:/Users/Prime/AppData/Local/npm-cache/_npx/1e7f6d9597241db0/node_modules/@deepseek-ai/dsh-subprocess-local/lib/index.js:693:75)
    at Socket.<anonymous> (file:///C:/Users/Prime/AppData/Local/npm-cache/_npx/1e7f6d9597241db0/node_modules/@deepseek-ai/dsh-subprocess-local/lib/index.js:862:14)
    at Socket.emit (node:events:518:28)
    ...
Node.js v22.17.0
```

The process hosting the web GUI (all sessions) died. The filename's spill counter (`-1-`) shows this was the server's *first* spill, ~83 minutes into the run — any subprocess whose output first exceeds the in-memory cap after the dir disappears triggers it.

## Root cause

- `lib/index.js:648-651` — the spill dir is created **once per process** via `mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), "dsh-subprocess-"))` and cached in `defaultSpillDir`. The module imports no `mkdir`, so it can never be recreated.
- `lib/index.js:710-721` — `spillAll()` calls `openSync(this.spillFile, "wx", 384)` and `writeSync(...)` with **no error handling**, synchronously inside a stream `data` handler (`push()` at `:693`, wired at `:862`). Any failure escapes as an uncaught exception and takes down the whole server, not just the affected subprocess.
- Nothing in any `@deepseek-ai/*` package deletes these dirs — a source-wide grep finds only the `mkdtemp` line, the per-file name template, and dependency pins. So an external `%TEMP%` cleaner (Storage Sense, AV, manual sweep) removing the *live* dir mid-session guarantees this crash on the next spill.
- Adjacent observation: stale `dsh-subprocess-*` dirs are never pruned; six accumulated on this machine over 10 days of normal use.

## Measured volume impact (Windows 11, 2026-09-29) — separate from the crash above

The same never-pruned behaviour was measured on a second, larger spill root on the same machine
(`%LOCALAPPDATA%\dsh-temp`, alongside the `%TEMP%\dsh-subprocess-*` dirs described above):

| Measurement | Value |
|---|---|
| Directory size | **13.03 GB across 41,415 files** |
| Largest single spills | 7 files of **444-600 MB**, all dated the previous day |
| Free space on C: at that moment | **197 MB** (volume: 926 GB used of ~1 TB) |
| After reclaiming files older than 24 h | 37,033 files / 7.75 GB deleted; C: **16.9 GB -> 24.67 GB** |

Downstream effects while the volume sat at 197 MB — all on the *host*, none of them inside the
spill code:

- another long-running agent process on the machine failed **every** write with
  `OSError: [Errno 28] No space left on device`; its cron persistence, its rotating log handler
  and its gateway loop all broke and retried in a loop;
- the CodeGraph MCP daemon for a neighbouring project began failing keepalives and reconnecting,
  and a full index rebuild could not take its database lock at all (`EPERM ... database file is
  in use`) — which is what a full disk looks like from SQLite's side;
- operations that spill large output became the least reliable thing on the box.

One observation recorded but **not** re-verified, because our local remedy reclaimed the files
before it could be checked: a depth-limited listing showed the *same* 599.9 MB file name three
times in that tree, which may mean duplicate spills of one object rather than three distinct ones.

### What we did locally (not a substitute for upstream pruning)

`deploy/dsh-temp-reaper.ps1`: reports by default, deletes only files older than a threshold
(default 24 h) inside a directory whose leaf is exactly `dsh-temp`, skips locked files without
forcing them, prints denominators, appends one line per run; installed as a daily 03:00 task
ahead of the 03:30 reindex so space is reclaimed before anything needs to write.

### Ask

1. **Prune stale spills** on startup and/or on a timer, bounded by age and total size, like any
   other cache.
2. **Document the spill roots** (`%TEMP%\dsh-subprocess-*` and `%LOCALAPPDATA%\dsh-temp`) so an
   operator can bound them without reverse-engineering.
3. **Fix the uncaught-`ENOENT` path** described above — that is what turns any external cleanup,
   ours included, into a server-wide crash.

## Repro

1. `npx @deepseek-ai/dsh web`, open a session (the spill dir is created lazily once a spill-capped subprocess spawns).
2. Delete `%TEMP%\dsh-subprocess-*` while the server runs.
3. Produce any subprocess output exceeding the in-memory collection cap (e.g. a long-running session agent or an MCP child with chatty stdout). The server exits with the stack above.

## Suggested fix (applied and verified locally)

Wrap the spill path in try/catch; on failure degrade to the existing tail-only path via `discardSpill()` (which already sets `spillDisabled = true`), recreate the dir on `ENOENT` so later collectors can spill again, and warn once per process:

```diff
--- a/lib/index.js (spillAll)
+import { ..., mkdirSync, ... } from "node:fs";
 	spillAll(chunk) {
 		if (this.maxSpillBytes !== void 0 && this.total > this.maxSpillBytes) {
 			this.discardSpill();
 			return;
 		}
-		if (this.spillFd === void 0) {
-			this.spillFile = join(this.spillDir, `dsh-subprocess-${process.pid}-${++spillCounter}-${randomBytes(6).toString("hex")}-${this.label}.log`);
-			this.spillFd = openSync(this.spillFile, "wx", 384);
-			for (const prior of this.chunks) writeSync(this.spillFd, prior);
-		}
-		writeSync(this.spillFd, chunk);
+		try {
+			if (this.spillFd === void 0) {
+				this.spillFile = join(this.spillDir, `dsh-subprocess-${process.pid}-${++spillCounter}-${randomBytes(6).toString("hex")}-${this.label}.log`);
+				this.spillFd = openSync(this.spillFile, "wx", 384);
+				for (const prior of this.chunks) writeSync(this.spillFd, prior);
+			}
+			writeSync(this.spillFd, chunk);
+		} catch (error) {
+			this.discardSpill();
+			if (error?.code === "ENOENT") {
+				try { mkdirSync(this.spillDir, { recursive: true }); } catch {}
+			}
+			if (OutputCollector._spillWarned !== true) {
+				OutputCollector._spillWarned = true;
+				console.warn(`dsh-subprocess-local: output spill to ${this.spillDir} failed (${error?.code ?? error?.message ?? String(error)}); degraded to in-memory tail${error?.code === "ENOENT" ? " and recreated the spill dir" : ""}`);
+			}
+		}
